= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In the one-dimensional commutative convention, a <formal group law> over $R$ is a <formal power series> $F(X,Y)=X+Y+\text{terms of degree at least two}$ satisfying $F(X,0)=X$, $F(0,Y)=Y$, $F(X,Y)=F(Y,X)$ and $F(F(X,Y),Z)=F(X,F(Y,Z))$. The <formal inverse> is a series $I(T)=-T+O(T^2)$ with $F(T,I(T))=0$. For $R=\mathbb Z_p$ all these series converge on $p\mathbb Z_p$, which becomes a group with operation $F$.

Repeated formal addition gives the <multiplication isomorphism of a formal group law> $[n]_F(T)=nT+O(T^2)$. If $p\nmid n$, the coefficient $n$ is a unit in the <p-adic integers>. We can construct an inverse $H(T)=n^{-1}T+\sum_{j\geq2}h_jT^j$ by coefficient recursion. At degree $j$, the equation $[n]_F(H(T))=T$ has the form $nh_j+\text{known integral terms}=0$; dividing by the unit $n$ keeps $h_j$ integral. The inverse is two-sided by uniqueness of formal composition inverses. Since integral coefficients multiplied by successive powers of $t\in p\mathbb Z_p$ tend to zero, both series converge on that set and their formal identities hold there. This proves <prime-to-residue-characteristic multiplication on a formal group>:
$$
\boxed{[n]_F:F(p\mathbb Z_p)\xrightarrow{\sim}F(p\mathbb Z_p),\qquad p\nmid n.}
$$
The argument also applies at $p=2$ for odd $n$; it does not require a logarithm or torsion-freeness.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For an integral <minimal Weierstrass equation>, let $E_0(\mathbb Q_p)$ be the subgroup of points whose reduction is a smooth point of the reduced cubic. Its reduction is a homomorphism to the <smooth-locus group of a singular Weierstrass cubic>, or to the whole reduced <elliptic curve> in the good-reduction case. Define the <formal kernel of a minimal Weierstrass equation> by
$$
\boxed{E_1(\mathbb Q_p)=\ker\bigl(E_0(\mathbb Q_p)\longrightarrow\widetilde E_{\mathrm{ns}}(\mathbb F_p)\bigr).}
$$
It consists of the identity and the points reducing to the identity at infinity. For a nonidentity affine point this is equivalent to $v_p(x)=-2r$ and $v_p(y)=-3r$ for some positive integer $r$.

The <local parameter> $t=-x/y$, with $t(O)=0$, gives the group isomorphism
$$
\boxed{E_1(\mathbb Q_p)\cong\widehat E(p\mathbb Z_p),}
$$
where $\widehat E$ is the <formal group of an elliptic curve>. To see the local coordinates, put $w=-1/y$ in the <Weierstrass equation of an elliptic curve>. The resulting relation is $w=t^3+a_1tw+a_2t^2w+a_3w^2+a_4tw^2+a_6w^3$, and its unit linear coefficient gives a unique integral series $w(t)=t^3+O(t^4)$. Thus $x=t/w$ and $y=-1/w$ have valuations $-2v_p(t)$ and $-3v_p(t)$. The <chord-and-tangent group law> expressed in this parameter is precisely the integral <formal group law> $\widehat E$. This description includes bad reduction; only the smooth identity neighborhood is involved.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Direct substitution gives $y^2+y=x^3-x+6$ for both points. The displayed <Weierstrass equation of an elliptic curve> is minimal at every prime by the permitted assumption.

For $P$, neither $364$ nor $11737$ is divisible by three or five. Therefore
$$
v_3(x(P))=v_5(x(P))=-2,\qquad
v_3(y(P))=v_5(y(P))=-3.
$$
The <formal kernel of a minimal Weierstrass equation> description gives $P\in E_1(\mathbb Q_3)\cap E_1(\mathbb Q_5)$. Similarly, neither $2059$ nor $98783$ is divisible by three or seven, so $Q\in E_1(\mathbb Q_3)\cap E_1(\mathbb Q_7)$, with the same respective coordinate valuations $-2,-3$.

If a point $T\in E_1(\mathbb Q_p)$ had finite <order of a group element> $N=p^a n$ with $p\nmid n$, the point $p^aT$ would be killed by $n$. By <prime-to-residue-characteristic multiplication on a formal group>, multiplication by $n$ is injective on this kernel, so $p^aT=O$. Its order is therefore a power of $p$. Applying this at two distinct primes is the <two-prime formal-kernel test for nontorsion>: the order of $P$ would be both a power of three and of five, and the order of $Q$ both a power of three and of seven. Each would have to be the identity, although both are affine points. **Both $P$ and $Q$ have infinite order.**

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

We use the <canonical height pairing> normalization $\langle P,Q\rangle=(\widehat h(P+Q)-\widehat h(P)-\widehat h(Q))/2$, so that $\langle P,P\rangle=\widehat h(P)$. The supplied values give
$$
\boxed{\langle P,Q\rangle\approx\frac{21.98-6.61-7.87}{2}=3.75.}
$$
The convention without the factor $1/2$ instead gives the doubled pairing $B(P,Q)\approx7.50$; all independence conclusions are the same.

In our convention the <Gram matrix> is approximately
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}6.61&3.75\\3.75&7.87\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\det H\approx6.61\cdot7.87-3.75^2=37.9582>0.
$$
This positive margin is much larger than the rounding uncertainty. Even allowing each of the three stated heights an error of $0.01$, we have $a\geq6.60$, $b\geq7.86$ and $|c|\leq3.765$, giving $ab-c^2\geq6.60\cdot7.86-3.765^2=37.700775>0$. Thus the actual <canonical height pairing> matrix is <positive-definite>, not merely its rounded approximation.

If $rP+sQ=O$ for integers $r,s$, <bilinearity> and the <height parallelogram identity> give $0=\widehat h(rP+sQ)=ar^2+2crs+bs^2$. Positive definiteness forces $r=s=0$. This is the <rank certificate from rounded canonical heights>, and proves
$$
\boxed{\langle P,Q\rangle_{\mathrm{group}}\cong\mathbb Z\times\mathbb Z.}
$$
Here the last brackets denote the generated subgroup, not the scalar height pairing. The argument proves independence; it does not assert that these points generate the entire <Mordell-Weil group>.

= 4
{parent=Paper 27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <elliptic-curve discriminant> of $y^2=x^3-12x^2+20x$ is $16\cdot20^2\cdot(144-80)=409600=2^{14}5^2$. Thus three and eleven are primes of <good reduction>. Counting the reduced points gives four points over $\mathbb F_3$ and sixteen over $\mathbb F_{11}$. For example, the numbers of affine points above successive $x=0,1,2$ modulo three are $1,1,1$. Modulo eleven, for $x=0,\ldots,10$, they are $1,2,1,2,0,0,2,2,2,2,1$; add the point at infinity in each case.

The <reduction of torsion points on an elliptic curve> is injective on torsion prime to the residue characteristic. The three-primary rational torsion injects into the group of order sixteen at eleven, so is trivial; likewise the eleven-primary torsion injects into the group of order four at three. Every other primary component injects at both primes. Therefore the rational <torsion subgroup> has order dividing four. All four rational <2-torsion> points are visible, so
$$
\boxed{E(\mathbb Q)_{\mathrm{tors}}=\{O,(0,0),(2,0),(10,0)\}\cong(\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)^2.}
$$

The rational point $P=(1,3)$ lies on the curve. The tangent slope in the <elliptic-curve addition formula> is $(3-24+20)/6=-1/6$, giving $2P=(361/36,-323/216)$. The integral substitution $X=x-4$ changes the equation to $y^2=X^3-28X-48$; the doubled point has $X=217/36$. The <Nagell–Lutz theorem> says that a rational torsion point on this integral short equation has integral coordinates, so $2P$, and hence $P$, is nontorsion. **An infinite-order point is $(1,3)$.**

We prove the rank bound by <two-isogeny descent>. For $a=-12,b=20$, the <two-isogeny formula> gives the partner
$$
E':Y^2=X^3+24X^2+64X
$$
and maps $\phi:E\to E'$, $\psi:E'\to E$ with $\psi\phi=[2]$. The <two-torsion square-class homomorphism> $\alpha:E(\mathbb Q)\to\mathbb Q^*/(\mathbb Q^*)^2$ is $\alpha(x,y)=[x]$ away from $O,(0,0)$, with values $1$ and $[20]=[5]$ at those exceptional points. Its kernel is $\psi E'(\mathbb Q)$. The corresponding map $\alpha'$ on $E'$ has kernel $\phi E(\mathbb Q)$.

The <prime-support bound in two-isogeny descent> can be checked directly here. For a prime $\ell\nmid20$, if $v_\ell(x)>0$ then $x^2-12x+20$ is a unit, so $2v_\ell(y)=v_\ell(x)$ is even; if $v_\ell(x)<0$, the $x^3$ term has strictly least valuation and $2v_\ell(y)=3v_\ell(x)$ is even. Thus the square class of $x$ is supported only on two and five. Also $x(x-2)(x-10)\geq0$ forces $x\in[0,2]\cup[10,\infty)$, so nonzero $x$ is positive. The possible classes are $1,2,5,10$, and the four torsion points provide all of them:
$$
\alpha(O)=1,\quad\alpha((2,0))=2,\quad
\alpha((0,0))=5,\quad\alpha((10,0))=10.
$$
Consequently $\#\alpha(E(\mathbb Q))=4$.

The same valuation argument on $E'$ gives possible signed classes $1,-1,2,-2$, since its coefficient $b'=64$ has only the prime two. The identity gives class $1$, and $(-4,8)\in E'(\mathbb Q)$ gives class $-1$. For $d=\pm2$, the <two-isogeny descent quartic> is
$$
W^2=dU^4+24U^2V^2+(64/d)V^4,
$$
with integral coprime $U,V$ after scaling a rational solution. If $U$ is odd, its right side is $2$ or $10$ modulo sixteen for $d=2$, and $14$ or $6$ for $d=-2$, according to the parity of $V$. None is a square. If $U=2u$ is even, $V$ is odd and the right side is $32(\pm u^4+3u^2V^2\pm V^4)$. The bracket is odd for either parity of $u$, so its <2-adic valuation> is five, again impossible for a square. The <signed divisor-two obstruction for an isogeny covering> excludes both classes. Hence $\alpha'(E'(\mathbb Q))=\{1,-1\}$.

For completeness, the index correction in the <two-isogeny index formula over a number field> is one: $\ker\psi=\{O,(0,0)\}$ and $\phi((2,0))=(0,0)$, so the whole kernel lies in $\phi E(\mathbb Q)$. Thus
$$
[E(\mathbb Q):2E(\mathbb Q)]=\#\alpha(E(\mathbb Q))\,\#\alpha'(E'(\mathbb Q))=4\cdot2=8.
$$
The <Mordell-Weil theorem> and the four rational <2-torsion> points identify this index as $2^{r+2}$. Therefore $2^{r+2}=8$ and
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}E(\mathbb Q)=1.}
$$
This is the <rank-one elliptic curve with roots zero two and ten>. We have exhibited a point of infinite order but have not needed to prove that it generates the free part.
